Z||000013||bocefas||12-31-2001||05:38 PM||||If it's any consolation for you, I won custody of my son (now 3) from his mother when he was less than a year old.  So it is possible, though difficult.  We lived together and the whole nine yards, then we split, with him living with her, and me getting him on my days off, and paying child support.  I have 3 days off a week, so I would go get him after my last day of work, and drop him off at bedtime the night before my first day back, and call on the days he was there.  This lasted a month.  Then pretty soon she realized that she was a single woman again, and started to ask me to keep him more and more, until like after 2 months, he was with me every night.  I kept both a calander and a file of receipts for everything, including money for support.  If she asked for extra, she got it, and I kept the receipt.  She thought she was pushing me over, and kept on going out and going out and going out...and the whole time I was just waiting for the right moment to sue her for custody.  She provided me with that opportunity when she told me that she was moving to California (from PA) because she met someone...HEH HEH HEH...<P>So anyway, to cut through the boring stuff, we went to court...the judge looked at everything I had and asked her a few questions and then looked right at her and told her in so many words that she was fu&*ing nuts if she thought that any judge anywere would take this child from his father, who was not only his only means of financial support, but also his "nurturing" parent as well.  She lost, I was awarded physical custody and was asked if I wanted child support!(which I declined).<P>She went to CA anyway, which goes to show how much she was really concerned about raising him.  Sometimes she goes months without calling him, but when she does I always treat her with respect and I never tell him anything bad about her.  She has come to realize she made a mistake, and know admits that he is better off with me.<P>So I guess the best thing I can tell you is:  You'll never get anywhere as far as custody and or visitation goes when you are states apart.  That may sound callous and cold, but that's not my intent.  The court looks at very few things actually when they determine custody.  They look at financial stability, emotional stability, and family background of both parents, and they look at where the child would have the best and most stable/consistent home life.  I can say that if I would have lost and she would have taken him to california, I would be typing this from there right now.  The only way you can succeed in my eyes and experience is to be where he is, go where he goes, and play nice with her until she screws up enough to enable you to win.  The biggest(and often hardest)thing to remember is that if you are going to fight this fight, do it for him, not for you, and not to hurt her.  Search your feelings and decide where he is better off, let your conscience be your guide.  If you have to live in a tent in Wyoming, so be it, at least then you can establish a routine and regular relationship with him.  I'm not going to bullshit you, being a single Dad is the hardest thing I have ever done, but also the most rewarding.  I get tired of men getting the bum rap all of the time during custody battles, but it's just like anything else, a few deadbeats ruin it for everyone else.<P>I certainly hope this helps, sorry for the length of this.  Feel free to PM me if I pi$$ed you off or if you want to blow off steam about it.  Good Luck.||||reg||1
